# Coding Best Practices

## Code Quality

### Simple Over Complex
```javascript
// Good: Clear intent
const isActive = user.status === 'active';

// Bad: Unnecessary complexity
const isActive = (() => user?.status?.toLowerCase() === 'active')();
```

### Early Returns
```javascript
// Good: Fail fast
function processUser(user) {
  if (!user) return null;
  if (!user.email) return null;
  // Main logic here
}

// Bad: Nested conditions
function processUser(user) {
  if (user) {
    if (user.email) {
      // Main logic here
    }
  }
}
```

## Error Handling

Always handle errors explicitly with meaningful messages:
```javascript
// Good
try {
  const data = await fetchData();
  return processData(data);
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Failed to fetch data:', error);
  return defaultData;
}

// Good: Include context in error messages
throw new Error(`User ${userId} not found in database`);
```

## Naming

- Use descriptive names: `userAuthenticationToken` not `token`
- Prefix booleans: `isLoading`, `hasPermission`, `canEdit`
- Functions should read as actions: `getUser`, `validateEmail`, `formatCurrency`

## Function Design

### Single Responsibility
```javascript
// Good: One purpose each
function calculateTax(amount, rate) { return amount * rate; }
function formatCurrency(amount) { return `$${amount.toFixed(2)}`; }

// Bad: Multiple responsibilities in one function
function calculateAndFormat(amount, rate) { ... }
```

### Consistent Return Types
```javascript
// Good: Always returns User | null
function findUser(id) {
  return users.find(u => u.id === id) || null;
}
```

## Performance

- Profile before optimizing — never guess
- Use the right data structure:
```javascript
// Good: O(1) lookup
const userMap = new Map(users.map(u => [u.id, u]));
const user = userMap.get(userId);

// Bad for frequent lookups: O(n)
const user = users.find(u => u.id === userId);
```

## State Management

Prefer immutability — create new objects instead of mutating:
```javascript
// Good
const updatedUser = { ...user, name: newName };
const newItems = [...items, newItem];

// Bad
user.name = newName;
items.push(newItem);
```

## Testing

Write pure, testable functions:
```javascript
// Good: Pure, easy to test
function calculateDiscount(price, percentage) {
  return price * (1 - percentage / 100);
}

// Bad: Depends on DOM and globals — hard to test
function calculateDiscount() {
  const price = document.getElementById('price').value;
  return price * (1 - globalDiscount / 100);
}
```

## Documentation

Comment on *why*, not *what*:
```javascript
// Good: Explains the reasoning
// Exponential backoff to avoid overwhelming the server during retries
const delay = Math.min(1000 * Math.pow(2, retryCount), 30000);

// Bad: States the obvious
// Multiply 1000 by 2 to the power of retryCount
const delay = 1000 * Math.pow(2, retryCount);
```

## Security

Never trust user input:
```javascript
// Good: Validate before use
const userId = parseInt(req.params.id, 10);
if (isNaN(userId) || userId <= 0) {
  return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid user ID' });
}

// Bad: Direct injection risk
const query = `SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ${req.params.id}`;
```

## Quick Decisions

When in doubt, choose in this order:
1. Readability
2. Maintainability
3. Simplicity
4. Explicitness

Remove dead code — delete commented-out blocks, unused imports, stray `console.log` calls.
